  "query_preview": "..Today...Today is setting up to be a slightly drier \nand less active day across northern AZ. Yesterday's surge in \nmoisture from the Gulf of California into western and northwestern\nAZ has declined in magnitude as the high to our east wobbles and \nthe flow turns suboptimal. PWAT values still range from near to \nslightly above normal for late August. And with clear skies this \nmorning leading to unrestricted surface heating, some modest \ndestabilization is still expected. Overall, expect isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms mostly along the higher terrain today.\n\nWednesday through Friday...The high continues to wobble and slowly \ndrift west, eventually becoming centered over northern AZ. This is \ntypically a poor setup for monsoon activity because it prevents the \ndeepest subtropical moisture from reaching northern AZ and can \nintroduce a cap. The overall forecast reflects this idea, \nmaintaining daily isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nmostly along the high terrain. With the reduction in moisture and \nabove normal heights at 500 mb, heat once again becomes a concern. \nThe lower elevations of the Grand Canyon and the lower elevations of \nYavapai county will have Extreme Heat Warnings in effect through \nthis period.\n\nSaturday through Monday...Long term ensemble guidance leans towards \nthe high shifting back to the east, while a deep trough approaches \nfrom the west. This could be a fairly active setup, and the forecast \ncalls for a 30-60% chance of precipitation across most of northern \nAZ each day. There is still plenty of room for the details to \nchange, and we will be watching this period closely with future \nforecast updates.",
